{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Then Jacob was greatly afraid and was distressed.</b> All the commentators set out to gather explanations for this fear, but found no sound reason why and on account of what he should have been afraid, after the Holy One, blessed be He, had promised him twice to protect him: the first time, in the vision of the ladder, it was said, ״וּשְׁמַרְתִּיךָ בְּכֹל אֲשֶׁר תֵּלֵךְ״ (Genesis 28:15); the second time, while he was in the house of Laban, it was said, ״שׁוּב אֶל אֶרֶץ מוֹלַדְתְּךָ וְאֶהְיֶה עִמָּךְ״ (Genesis 31:3). And what they explained — that he was afraid lest sin cause [the promise to be nullified] — reason refuses to accept this view, for it was those eight days since it was said to him in Laban's house, ״שׁוּב אֶל אֶרֶץ מוֹלַדְתְּךָ״ — and can it enter the mind to say that in the interim he became corrupt and committed a great sin that caused this promise to be nullified? And Maharso was greatly troubled by this difficulty." "And what seems close to me to say in resolving this doubt is along the path that our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Sotah 41b): Rabbi Elazar said: everyone who flatters his fellow, in the end falls into his hand, etc. And so too here, Jacob felt within himself that he had sinned in this, that he flattered the wicked man and said, ״כֹּה אָמַר עַבְדְּךָ יַעֲקֹב״ (Genesis 32:5), and so our Rabbis of blessed memory took Jacob to task, as is deduced (Genesis Rabbah 75:3): ״עֵשָׂו לְדַרְכּוֹ הוּא הוֹלֵךְ״, etc., ״וְיַעֲקֹב עָשָׂה קֹדֶשׁ חוֹל וְקָרָא לְעֵשָׂו אֲדוֹנִי״ — on account of the flattery he flattered the wicked man. Therefore he was afraid, for Jacob knew that such is the measure: everyone who flatters the wicked man, in the end falls into his hand; therefore it says, ״וַיִּירָא יַעֲקֹב״ (Genesis 32:8). And since Jacob undoubtedly had in his hand merits so very many that they would outweigh this sin, nevertheless it distressed him that they would deduct from his merits through the fact that he would be saved from Esau his brother; therefore it says, ״וַיֵּצֶר לוֹ״ (Genesis 32:8)." "And some say, ״וַיִּירָא יַעֲקֹב״ — lest Esau kill him; ״וַיֵּצֶר לוֹ״ — that he was distressed over the death of his father Isaac, for after all Esau said, ״יִקְרְבוּ יְמֵי אֵבֶל אָבִי וְאַהַרְגָה אֶת אָחִי״ (Genesis 27:41); therefore he reasoned that since Esau was coming out to meet him, his father had surely already died. Thus it is deduced in Sefer Toldot Yitzchak. And it seems to me to resolve thereby what was said above (Genesis 31:42), ״וּפַחַד יִצְחָק״, whereas here it says ״אֱלֹהֵי אָבִי יִצְחָק״ — but behold, the Holy One, blessed be He, does not join His name, blessed be He, upon the righteous during their lifetimes (Tanchuma, Toldot 7), so why did it say here ״אֱלֹהֵי אָבִי יִצְחָק״? Rather, because now he supposed that his father Isaac was dead, since Esau was coming out to meet him to kill him. And according to this, we have no need for Rashi's explanation here, where he explained regarding the two promises, ״אָנֹכִי בָּא״, etc." "And some say that the name Elohim, which denotes the attribute of justice, he mentioned specifically regarding his forefathers, for the merit of the fathers would suffice him to stand even in the attribute of justice; but his own merit is small, so that on its own he cannot stand except in the attribute of mercy — therefore it says ״HaShem הָאוֹמֵר אֵלַי״, but in the attribute of justice I cannot stand, for I do not have such great merits in my hand, for behold, ״קָטֹנְתִּי מִכָּל הַחֲסָדִים״ (Genesis 32:11) — for I received reward far greater than my deeds, for the kindnesses of El, blessed be He, overpowered all my deeds; it follows that all my deeds are smaller than His kindnesses, as it is written (Psalms 117:2), ״כִּי גָבַר עָלֵינוּ חַסְדּוֹ״."], :book :genesis, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 32, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:32:01.648500649Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}